# Every rule beside the grain it declares and the grain its rows actually have. One row per rule.
#
# The contract's columns name a subject many rules do not examine
#   `checks/all.sqlc` returns `(rule, filing, layer, ...)` and says *which layer of which filing
#   it is*. Wherever the examined item is finer than a layer (a part, a slack or a claim),
#   `(filing, layer)` is not a key and the item's identity survives only inside the prose
#   `detail`. `examples/soundness/main.rs` §4 prints the tally; `claim` is the third subject and
#   the largest of the three.
#
# Two things called one bag
#   `invariance.sqlc` says `checks/all.sqlc` is a bag because *several items per layer produce
#   the same sentence*, and that is true of only some rules. For the others every row says
#   something different, and the bag is a grain artifact rather than a repetition one. Its
#   count-don't-subtract repair is right for both, which is worth knowing: the repair was more
#   general than its stated reason.
#
# The law is one-directional
#   That is not a weakness. A rule declaring `layer` must have exactly one row per
#   `(filing, layer)`; a second row means it silently changed what it examines and no other law
#   here would see it, because the count moves and nothing says what the count is of. The
#   converse cannot be asserted: a part-grained rule may legitimately see one part per layer in a
#   corpus that happens to file one, and accusing it would be reading luck as a declaration.
#
# A unique key is not a key that resolves
#   That is why this file has a second arm. `unresolvable` counts the rows whose
#   `(filing, layer)` names no filed layer. A NULL is not counted: a rule about a composition
#   legitimately has no layer, and the contract's column is nullable for exactly that. What is
#   counted is a value that looks like a layer and is not, which is the state a reader and every
#   join downstream cannot detect.
#
# The arm the multiplicity one cannot be
#   `finer_than_declared` asks whether the key is unique per row; `unresolvable` asks whether the
#   key resolves. A rule putting an address like `pm:nameplate/pm:amount claim 7` in the `layer`
#   column satisfies the multiplicity arm by construction, because a value unique per row is one
#   row per key. What makes the column wrong is exactly what makes that arm pass.
#
# Zero is a repairable state
#   It is not a property of the contract. A rule whose subject is finer than a layer reports the
#   finer item's identity in `detail` and its own layer in the column, because anything joining
#   a verdict to a layer keeps only the pairs that resolve and drops the rest in silence.
-- checks/roster.sqlc's declared subject against checks/all.sqlc's multiplicity and its key.
SELECT r.slug,
       r.subject                                     AS declares,
       count(c.rule)                                 AS rows,
       count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer))           AS layers,
       count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer, c.detail)) AS sentences,
       r.subject = 'layer'
         AND count(c.rule) <> count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer)) AS finer_than_declared,
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.layer IS NOT NULL AND l.layer IS NULL) AS unresolvable
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.rule = r.rule
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l ON l.filing = c.filing AND l.layer = c.layer
GROUP BY r.slug, r.subject
